{"id":"promoted-into-one-holder-class","title":"Moved into a class in which the person is the only holder","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Who moved from one City job class into another in which they were the only holder citywide, was the class empty beforehand, and what did the move do to their pay?","finding":"54 followed employees moved, between fiscal 2018 and 2026, into a job class in which they were the only paid holder in the City that year. In 42 cases nobody had been paid in the class the year before, and in 22 of those the person’s rate of pay rose by a tenth or more. The middle rise across all 54 moves was 10.2%.","how":"City payroll file. For each followed name and fiscal year from 2018: the main job is in a class whose only paid holder citywide that year is this person, and the person’s main job the year before was a different class. The rate of pay is regular salary divided by hours, as a yearly figure. Moves into a class that was empty the year before come first, by the rise in dollars. Board seats, elected offices and the Superior Court are left out.","limits":"Succeeding a retiring specialist or supervisor is the ordinary way into a one-person class, and an empty class may simply have been vacant while the post was advertised. The pattern becomes a question when the class was created or revived in the same year and the pay rose sharply. The rate is per hour paid, and the file shows full-time firefighters paid for about 2,904 hours a year against 2,080 for office classes, so a move from a fire rank into a chief’s post shows a larger rise here than in salary. The classification record would show when and why the class was opened; the job announcement would show whether anyone else could apply.","columns":[{"k":"person","t":"Name on the payroll","type":"person"},{"k":"year","t":"Fiscal year of the move","type":"year"},{"k":"toJob","t":"Into","type":"text"},{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"fromJob","t":"From","type":"text"},{"k":"rat